abstract='''Weekly rainfall forecasts and longer accumulations should agree, but statistical calibration can break their aggregation identity. We test whether climate information added to aggregate forecasts provides value beyond calibrated weekly forecasts. The experiment uses 1060 ECMWF hindcast origins during 2000 to 2019 in four Australian domains, verified against Australian Gridded Climate Data. A factorial design separates climate predictors from additional fortnightly forecasts, with nested year-block validation excluding neighbouring years. Climate information changes reconciled 28-day continuous ranked probability score (CRPS) by −0.31% of climatological score across domains (95% year-bootstrap interval −0.70 to 0.06%); adding fortnights changes it by 0.003% (−0.16 to 0.16%). Neither contrast passes multiplicity adjustment. An exploratory northern comparison favours climate-informed reconciliation over rainfall-only weekly aggregation by 0.54 mm (0.15 to 0.95 mm), mainly in summer. However, adding the same predictors to weekly forecasts also improves the northern score. The difference between climate-informed weekly aggregation and reconciliation is uncertain, and the weekly approach performs better in a separate 2015–2019 forward evaluation. Intercept-only and permuted-year controls do not reproduce the northern improvement, while individual-index results provide no unique attribution to MJO. A further power-map adjustment preserves coherence but increases held-out CRPS. These results support local evaluation of climate information while leaving the additional predictive value of reconciliation uncertain.'''
section('## Abstract','Keywords:', '## Abstract\n\n'+abstract)
t=t.replace('These relationships can affect both accumulated amounts and the sequence of wet and dry weeks.','These climate influences affect both accumulated amounts and the sequence of wet and dry weeks.')
start='The question addressed here is whether climate information introduced only at the aggregate levels improves coherent subseasonal rainfall forecasts when the weekly distributions are held fixed.'
a=t.index(start);b=t.index('## 2 Data and experimental design',a)
t=t[:a]+'''The remaining question for this application is what direct aggregate forecasts contribute after the weekly distributions are held fixed. A lower score after reconciliation can reflect useful aggregate predictors, additional hierarchy nodes or changes in forecast dependence. Climate information introduced at the weekly scale may also supply the same benefit through simple aggregation. Distinguishing these explanations requires comparisons that hold the other forecast components constant.

We therefore test two hypotheses: climate-informed aggregate forecasts improve on their rainfall-only counterparts, and adding independently calibrated fortnights contributes further value. Both are evaluated against a common sum of fixed weekly forecasts. Additional controls then allow climate information to enter the weeks, remove or permute its predictors, and change the dependence construction. Error and uncertainty diagnostics connect these comparisons to rainfall seasonality. A power-map experiment examines the separate question of adjusting accumulated uncertainty while preserving coherent, nonnegative trajectories.

'''+t[b:]
