newmethods='''### 3.6 Additional controls and robustness checks

The additional controls are exploratory and were specified after the primary factorial results. First, the H1 adjustment is fitted to the four weekly means and those forecasts are summed. This tests whether aggregate reconciliation adds value beyond climate-informed weekly calibration. Second, aggregate intercept-only adjustments use exp(β₀), with the same bounds and penalty as H1. Northern controls retain MJO, Niño3.4, DMI or SAM separately. Three fixed placebos permute predictor-year blocks within each fitting set while retaining approximate calendar alignment; responses and held-out observations are never permuted into the fit. A further northern model uses monthly indices lagged one month. That sensitivity uses archived index values and is not a reconstruction of real-time releases.

The seven-node H0/H1 experiment is repeated with matched 11-sample ECC and historical ranks. Northern H0/H1 models are also refitted under both alternative archive corrections. A separate forward evaluation fits using 2000 to 2014, excluding target windows ending after 2014, and evaluates 2015 to 2019. Its parameters, covariance and templates use only the earlier period. All contrasts retain their own coherent weekly baseline.

Small-sample checks use BCa intervals (Efron 1987), circular moving blocks of two and three years, annual differences and leave-one-year influence. The power-map audit distinguishes loss in its fitting windows, application to central-month inner samples and outer validation. A northern spatial sensitivity repeats the models in all four 1.5° forecast cells inside N, using newly extracted AGCD observations at the same cell boundaries; no cell is selected from its score.

'''
