section('### 4.5 Covariance and tail diagnostics','### 4.6 Robustness', '''### 4.5 Covariance and upper-tail diagnostics

The covariance-based gain predictor is systematically optimistic (Fig. 7). All 16 domain-season cases lie below the agreement line. Observed minus predicted gain averages −4.96 percentage points for linear reconciliation and −5.21 points for nonnegative sample means; fitted slopes are 0.12 and 0.07. Spearman correlations are 0.41 (95% year-bootstrap interval −0.03 to 0.61) and 0.34 (−0.06 to 0.47). Northern summer has about 9% predicted and 6% observed gain, whereas winter has about 22% predicted gain and a small observed deterioration. These relationships offer little support for selecting reconciliation from estimated covariance alone.

Tail scores also differ from overall CRPS. H1 seven-node threshold-weighted CRPS is 2.274, 3.756, 2.453 and 1.611 mm in N, NE, SE and SW, compared with bottom-up values of 2.295, 3.750, 2.475 and 1.619 mm. Corresponding 28-day Brier skill scores are 0.218, 0.170, 0.225 and 0.101, against bottom-up values of 0.187, 0.179, 0.218 and 0.107. Thus the forecast has skill relative to the empirical event reference in every domain, while adding aggregate information changes that skill in different directions. Table 4 and Supplementary Table S7 report the probability scores and descriptive intervals.''')
section('### 4.6 Robustness','## 5 Discussion','''### 4.6 Robustness and the power-map diagnostic

The northern H1 minus bottom-up 28-day difference remains favourable after complete factorial refitting under positive-part increments (−0.507 mm) and signed predictors (−0.493 mm), compared with −0.541 mm under the primary correction. Changing the sample size from 200 to 1000 gives differences of −0.538 to −0.527 mm across the three-seed averages. Table 7 summarises these core sensitivities; full results appear in Supplementary Tables S1 and S5.

Matched dependence controls retain a northern climate effect. H1 minus H0 CRPS is −0.414 mm under 11-sample ECC (95% interval −0.837 to −0.018 mm) and −0.382 mm under matched historical ranks (−0.774 to −0.013 mm). These comparisons use identical marginal quantiles within each dependence scheme. Absolute finite-sample scores and interval widths differ, so they support the local direction of the climate contrast rather than equivalence of the dependence models. The earlier homogeneous ECC-minus-historical differences are +0.038, +0.017, −0.061 and −0.076 mm across the four domains, with all intervals including zero.

BCa and annual-influence checks clarify the northern result (Fig. 9). H1 improves on bottom-up in 14 of 20 northern years, while the domain-averaged contrast is favourable in 12 years. Omitting any one northern year leaves the mean difference between −0.627 and −0.451 mm. Its BCa interval is −0.966 to −0.174 mm, while the northern H1-minus-H0 interval includes zero under two- and three-year blocks. The domain-averaged H1-minus-bottom-up interval also includes zero with three-year blocks (−1.002 to 0.011% of climatological CRPS). These descriptive checks leave the primary multiplicity-adjusted conclusion unchanged.

The power map narrows central 80% intervals (Fig. 8). Coverage changes from 84.1 to 81.8% in N, 80.9 to 79.1% in NE, 80.8 to 79.6% in SE and 82.3 to 80.3% in SW. N, SE and SW move closer to 80%; NE crosses it with the same absolute discrepancy. Outer 28-day CRPS increases by 0.425 mm in N (0.180 to 0.665 mm), 0.402 mm in NE (0.234 to 0.572 mm), 0.070 mm in SE (−0.049 to 0.206 mm) and 0.043 mm in SW (−0.059 to 0.162 mm).

The inner audit identifies an objective-population mismatch. Every fitted map reduces penalised loss in its 91-day fitting window. Average unpenalised changes there are −0.219 and −0.096 mm in N and NE, yet applying each map only to its central calendar month raises inner CRPS by 0.173 and 0.225 mm. Inner-to-outer covariance differences are measurable, with mean relative Frobenius differences of 0.10 to 0.16 across domains, but outer covariance shift cannot alone explain deterioration already present on inner application samples. This result concerns this monthly power-map construction. All transformed trajectories remain nonnegative and coherent; updating only the total instead leaves mean aggregation discrepancies of 3.59, 2.67, 1.60 and 1.54 mm.

### 4.7 Added-information controls and forward evaluation

Adding climate information to weekly forecasts lowers northern bottom-up CRPS from 21.087 to 20.723 mm (Table 5). H1 aggregate reconciliation scores 20.546 mm; its difference from climate-informed weekly aggregation is −0.177 mm, with an interval from −0.394 to 0.046 mm. This comparison provides no clear additional scoring advantage for reconciliation. In NE, SE and SW, climate-informed weekly aggregation scores 17.416, 11.490 and 8.739 mm.

An aggregate intercept alone barely changes the northern H0 score: the difference is +0.001 mm (Table 6). The three permuted-year models score 21.118 to 21.229 mm, compared with 20.918 mm for H0 and 20.546 mm for H1. The improvement is therefore not reproduced by these extra-flexibility controls. In the single-index experiments, DMI and MJO give favourable mean changes from H0, Niño3.4 is uncertain and SAM is adverse. DMI scores 20.423 mm and MJO 20.671 mm, but neither is clearly better than the full H1 model. These exploratory comparisons identify predictive associations under the specified models, without isolating a physical cause. One-month monthly-index lags score 20.616 mm, a difference from H1 of +0.070 mm (−0.254 to 0.416 mm).

The strictly forward northern evaluation gives H0, H1, bottom-up and climate-informed weekly aggregation CRPS values of 19.134, 18.639, 19.282 and 18.225 mm, respectively. The climate effect persists in direction, but weekly climate calibration performs better than aggregate reconciliation in these five evaluation years. Forward H1 changes from bottom-up are smaller in the other domains and adverse in NE. Table 5 retains all four domains; this short forward period is a sensitivity check rather than a new primary test.

SPATIAL_RESULT_V3''')
section('## 5 Discussion','## 6 Conclusions','''## 5 Discussion

The additional controls sharpen the distinction between information and coherence. A larger hierarchy has little average effect, and climate-informed aggregates help mainly in the exploratory northern comparison. However, some of that information can also be introduced through the weekly marginals and retained by simple summation. In the forward northern evaluation, that simpler route has the lower CRPS. The experiment therefore supports testing where climate information enters the forecast, rather than assigning the full gain over rainfall-only bottom-up to reconciliation.

Northern rainfall provides a meteorological context for these differences. The monsoon season and MJO affect rainfall occurrence and persistence (Wheeler et al. 2009; Cowan et al. 2024), while Indian Ocean conditions can influence rainfall across the seasonal cycle (Risbey et al. 2009). Both MJO and DMI groups improve the northern mean score in the fitted controls, with uncertainty about their relative contribution. The analysis neither separates overlapping climate influences nor identifies a causal pathway. The absence of a stronger result with shorter monthly lags also shows that fresher archived indices alone do not resolve the prediction problem.

The weak southwestern response is consistent with the broader need to verify incremental predictive value locally. Firth et al. (2024) found that representing the SAM–rainfall relationship did not remove limitations in ACCESS-S2 rainfall skill. Reid et al. (2024) likewise reported geographically conditional value for moisture transport. These studies concern different products and spatial supports, but they motivate the same practical check: whether an auxiliary predictor improves on information already present in the forecast.

Dependence determines how marginal rainfall uncertainty accumulates (Bellier et al. 2023). The historical template produces broad northern bottom-up intervals, yet a northern H1-minus-H0 improvement also appears under ECC. Thus the climate contrast is not confined to the original historical template. Its size and absolute calibration still depend on sample construction. The power-map diagnostic further shows how a loss reduction over one seasonal population can fail when the map is issued over another. Matching the fitting and application populations deserves attention alongside preserving the sums.

The variance calculations have a narrower role than direct verification. Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance, whereas the issued samples undergo nonnegative projection and are evaluated with distributional scores. The optimistic seasonal gain predictions demonstrate the practical gap. Likewise, bottom-up optimality for particular temporally aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) does not determine the result for separately calibrated rainfall marginals.

The results concern one ECMWF hindcast collection, four main domains and a northern support sensitivity. They establish neither continent-wide skill nor transfer to another operational system. Within that scope, a useful workflow is to compare rainfall-only and climate-informed weekly aggregation before adding direct aggregate models, then verify probabilities and scale-specific losses for every coherent product.''')
section('## 6 Conclusions','## Data availability','''## 6 Conclusions

In this ECMWF rainfall experiment, climate information affects forecast scores more than adding two fortnightly nodes, but none of the three domain-averaged primary comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. A favourable northern result is exploratory, concentrated in summer and sensitive to how climate information enters the forecast. Climate-informed weekly aggregation recovers part of the twenty-year gain and performs better than aggregate reconciliation in the separate forward evaluation.

Intercept and permuted-year controls do not reproduce the northern improvement. MJO and DMI controls show favourable predictive associations, with no clear attribution to one climate mode. Covariance-based gain predictions are systematically optimistic, and this monthly power-map construction increases held-out CRPS despite coherent trajectories and narrower intervals.

Temporal coherence ensures that rainfall amounts agree across forecast horizons. Its additional forecasting value must be assessed against coherent alternatives using the information available at each scale. The new controls make that comparison explicit and support local, distribution-based evaluation of climate predictors and reconciliation.''')
